A||N||17||MikeE||Cadillac V8/auto?||||5||
Z||000000||MikeE||05-31-2000||08:51 PM||||I originally posted this as a reply but I wanted to see what other members thought about this so I'm reposting it.  Due to the heavy traffic in Hawaii and the bad rep of Fiero clutches, I too decided I would rather have an auto. A year of cross country trucking on the mainland with a 13sp (along with a Mack triplex 3 stick as well) in my younger days cured me of any love of shifting. However, I would much rather have a 4sp OD than the stock 3sp. Instead of trying to mate the trans to a chevy V8, I've thought about searching for a 91 Cadillac, which had a 4.9L V8/4sp OD auto with 200 hp & 275 lb-ft of torque, and swapping in the entire engine/trans/ECM. That way I would have a modern engine with computer controlled electronic fuel injection along with the sound and low end torque of a V8 and the benefits of an automatic OD trans (which I believe is a 4T60E). If I find a suitable wrecked donor car it would even come at a reasonable price, something that cannot be said about a Northstar or even a 3800 SC. I know there isn't a large aftermarket for this engine but mild mods like freeing up the exhaust and a K & N filter should bump it a little. Other normal mods such as porting, extrude honing the intake, having a new chip burned, etc. should also work well. The info from TerryK about the 3:43 final drive in the vans is enticing since the Cadi's ratio is not that low. However, the lighter weight of the Fiero may make that moot. Any thoughts?<P>                Aloha, Mike<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||05-31-2000||09:10 PM||||If I'm not mistaken, that's the best way to do a Northstar conversion, by swapping in a complete engine and tranny combo plus ECM, wiring, etc. from the same donor car. Let me know if you need any links to websites or go to Fiero Secrets and look at their links. It's an awesome swap, but expensive, buying a whole wrecked car might be wise.||||reg||1
Z||000002||lowCG||06-01-2000||01:42 AM||||The motor you're talking about is all aluminum,right,pre-northstar?<BR>If so,that motor would be nice in an automatic Fiero,they only made it for a short amount of time also,so you may have to rebuild if you can't find one with low miles,and parts may be expensive(?)||||reg||1
Z||000003||MikeE||06-01-2000||05:06 AM||||Yes, I am talking about the pre-northstar engine.  The 200HP 4.9L version was offered in 91-92, the 155HP 4.5L version in 88-89, which was upped to 180HP in 90 along with a premium fuel requirement.  From 85-87 it was a 125-130HP 4.1L which did not have a stellar reputation but from what I gather, the major complaints were that it was underpowered and had a history of coolant and oil leaks.  Before that the engines were mounted longitudinally so the transmissions are not suitable for a Fiero swap.<P>You're right, it was all aluminum and very compact, much more so than the northstar.  Due to the nature of most Cadillac drivers the cars have had relatively easy lives and often have low miles.  They are fairly plentiful since they came in both coupe/sedan de Villes and Eldorado/Sevilles (i.e. all Cadillacs in those years).  The owners tend to want a high price for well kept cars so a wreck would be best but here in Hawaii I can log on to the insurance auction website and find out what cars are available.  The biggest danger here is that they are very popular as taxis and those cars have high miles but they are easy to spot.  Conversely, the taxi drivers tend to maintain their cars very well since their livelihood depends on it.<P>The 4.9 had an electronically controlled trans and I don't know if that would add to the swap's difficulty but it gives the opportunity to modify the trans' shifting characteristics through electronic manipulation.  It also had an interesting feature in the higher level cars which I doubt could adapted to the Fiero.  Its shocks had 3 levels of firmness that varied according to the car's speed-soft to 40mph, med to 60, and firm beyond that.  I think it would be fairly easy to get 220-230HP which would  feel strong in a Fiero, especially with firmed up shifts.  I'll bet the V8 would sound nice through the Fiero V6 muffler or an aftermarket model since my V6 sounded pretty good.  The safety check guys didn't want to pass me, saying the muffler must be burned out on the inside, even though it looked perfect (it was kind of loud).  It would be a fun swap but a lot of work since I would have to fab everything and figure out the electronics myself.<P>Batboy, even though this is not a Northstar swap, I could probably gain useful info from looking at their procedures so if you have any links I would really appreciate having them.  Thanks in advance.<P>Aloha, Mike.||||reg||6
Z||000004||MikeE||06-01-2000||05:18 AM||||Batboy, even though this is not a Northstar swap (pre-Northstar engine), I could probably gain useful info from looking at their procedures so if you have any links I would really appreciate having them.  Thanks in advance.<P>Aloha, Mike.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by batboy:<BR><B>If I'm not mistaken, that's the best way to do a Northstar conversion, by swapping in a complete engine and tranny combo plus ECM, wiring, etc. from the same donor car. Let me know if you need any links to websites or go to Fiero Secrets and look at their links. It's an awesome swap, but expensive, buying a whole wrecked car might be wise.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||5
Z||000005||phillip||06-01-2000||09:39 AM||||i've asked about this engine too for the same reasons stated a few weeks back. i e-mailed v8 archie to see what he knew about them. he replied quite a bit what do i want to know? i pretty much asked for general info since this swap isn't talked about much. i never got a second e-mail from him, but i guess he knows something.<P>a fews years back one of the hot rod type mags did a short blurb on a fwd monte carlo with this swap. they didn't mention any performance parts though. there was also that pylon python site on the net, but i can't find it any more.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||06-01-2000||10:17 AM||||Here it is:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.azstarnet.com/~gduncan/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.azstarnet.com/~gduncan/</A> <P>It might not run with a Northstar, but it would be plenty fast and I would think serviceability would be easier.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Uaana||06-01-2000||12:40 PM||||Just my 2c, but..<BR>Why not go with a 3.4 swap? Seems like the performance numbers are very similar and the parts/swap info are a lot more plentiful.<BR>If I remember right the 3.4 that is being done down at the fierofactory is putting out around 200hp.||||reg||5
Z||000008||Monkeyman||06-01-2000||03:00 PM||||MikeE--There's another Hawaiian here on the forum.  Do you know each other?  I don't know anything about motors so I can't help you there, but a 13 is a cake walk.  And see if this rings a bell.  Directions for shifting a Mack TriPlex:  1)  Hook your arm through the steering wheel.  2)  Whatever you do, don't miss a gear. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||06-01-2000||08:43 PM||||Ok, you can tell I don't know diddly-squat about Caddy engines. However, I was thinking the 4.9 front wheel drive motor was a direct bolt in as far as the bellhousing to tranny goes. Of course I could be wrong about that too. You still have to figure out motor mounts, wiring, and 2 dozen other little things. Guess I don't have many Caddy conversion links but here are a couple:<P> <A HREF="http://www.designonesystems.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.designonesystems.com/</A> <P> <A HREF="http://www.inetport.com/~van_blok/northstar.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.inetport.com/~van_blok/northstar.html</A> <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dan86gt3.8||06-02-2000||03:27 AM||||whoa, whoa, whoa chief.  Are you talking about the 3.4 v6.  thats only what 160 hp thats a big 30 hp increase.  Don't get me wrong swaps are awesome in any shape or form.  But you would get alott more out of 3.8's and caddy v8's.  Ohhh as far as the extrude hone, make sure it has a metal intake, cause some are liable to be plastic like mine <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||MikeE||06-04-2000||09:49 AM||||I don't know if anyone will see this but thanks for all the responses and the links.  When I first started thinking about this, I was also considering the 3.4 DOHC and I put the question out on some other forums.  I got several responses suggesting I consider the 3.4 pushrod V6 since it would be a more straightforward swap, being virtually a bolt in, and newer versions claim to have 180HP.  However, I still favor the Cad 4.9 V8.  The 3.4 is mostly available in late model cars so it will be hard to find a good deal and most of them have OBD II which I want to avoid.  Only a few cars have the 180hp version, most are 160 or 170, and I think only the first few years it was in the Camaro are OBD I, although the Camaro was 180hp.   Also, the 60 degree V6 is not very smooth compared to a V8 or the balance shafted 3800.   It turns out that the Cad 4.9 was available 91-95 as long as the owner didn't opt for the Northstar and many de Ville owners did not, being, in general, an older, fairly sedate crowd, which also means the engines led a fairly easy life.  In addition, the owners were usually affluent and could afford to pay for regular maintainence and they often didn't put on a lot of miles.  It also turns out thet it will bolt right up to the Fiero's Muncie/Getrag 5sp as long as the bell housing is slightly clearanced for the starter which is mounted slightly higher on the Cad and you do need a custom flywheel.  Adapting the electronics won't be easy but it won't be any harder than any other computer controlled engine swap since others have done it and info is out there.  In addition, Team Python reports that the all aluninum engine w/o ac weighs the same as the 2.8 V6 w/ac so it's virtually the same weight.  I think with a K & N, a freer exhaust, and maybe some power pulleys, 225hp & 300lb-ft of torque are easily attainable and when hooked to a 5sp, that will feel very strong in a Fiero.  Bolting on a centrifical supercharger wouldn't be that hard either.||||reg||6
Z||000012||phillip||06-04-2000||10:43 AM||||i'd be interested in any thing you find out. as i said i had been asking a few questions about this engine myself. i know that if i get the chance to do a swap it will depend on opportunity and if this engine pops up i want to know all i can. it seems like it would be higher on the list of swap candidates but seems to have the least info concerning it.<P>a few months back i said it would be good if more of the manufacturers joined in these posts for expert input. this is one of those times.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||06-04-2000||02:14 PM||||220 hp and 300 tq should feel strong in a Fiero??? That's an understatement! That's about what my current 350 Chevy engine in my Fiero puts out, maybe slightly less and it is awesome. It will get me into trouble for sure. Jump on the throttle and it flat out takes off.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 06-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||mrfixit58||06-04-2000||07:48 PM||||I was corresponding with one of the guys building the Pylon Python a while back. I asked about some of the mods he has done to the motor. Basically, accept for some port matching and the Allante' intake, the motor is pretty much stock. He said that except for the Vipers, their car was about the quickest on the track. I asked if they had any more mods planned for the motor and he said they were looking at modifying some Ford rocker arms to up the ratio (no performance cam available). The project seems like a good one.<P>I was contemplating this conversion but if you look at the numbers and the stuff that is readily available, it's tough to beat a SBC. According to V-8 Archie, the SBC added 150 pounds with cast iron heads. Installing the motor with aluminum heads will cut an additional ~60+ pounds off of that. Move the battery up front and now the rear is +50 and the front is +40 (weight bias would be bassically stock). It's very easy to add a few HP to a SBC to compensate for just 90 pounds. Heck, I could probably compensate for most of that by going on a diet.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>Roy||||reg||10
Z||000015||batboy||06-04-2000||09:22 PM||||No doubt about it, more hipo goodies for the SBC then you can shake a stick at. As fast as my Batmobile is now, I'm beginning to wonder if I really need a 400+ hp roller 350 engine like I'm building for it right now. Guess it really will be a Vette killer then, if I don't wrap it around a telephone pole first. Unless you're really a major hipo gear head (like me), that 4.9 Caddy engine will be more than enough power for most people...and certainly more power than some need. Chevy small block parts are cheap and everywhere though.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Jay||06-06-2000||04:18 PM||||I'm in the middle of a northstar conversion and I've got a page up <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A>  I'll answer any questions that I can.  I've never seen a 4.9 engine swap but I can tell you that a Northstar is not that difficult, just time consuming.||||reg||1
Z||000017||mrfixit58||06-06-2000||04:52 PM||||Jay,<P>I would be courious in the costs of the swap. Not really interested in the cost of the motor(since that will vary) but more in the wiring harness, axles, sensor swap, hubs, etc.<P>Roy||||reg||1
